00:09:16,298 --> 00:09:17,438
And those that do-

268
00:09:17,438 --> 00:09:18,484
- Will wish he were dead.

269
00:09:18,484 --> 00:09:20,668
- His sloppy speech.

270
00:09:20,668 --> 00:09:21,578
Do you know-

271
00:09:21,578 --> 00:09:23,462
- That I have little
bunnies painted on my knees?

272
00:09:23,462 --> 00:09:24,463
I do.

273
00:09:24,463 --> 00:09:25,646
- Most of the speakers we have heard

274
00:09:25,646 --> 00:09:27,304
can be traced to one thing.

275
00:09:27,304 --> 00:09:28,137
- Tight under things.

276
00:09:28,137 --> 00:09:30,720
- Practically all of us
would be better speakers

277
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:34,056
if it were not for this one big fault.

278
00:09:34,056 --> 00:09:35,639
- Red car-lessness?

279
00:09:36,792 --> 00:09:37,625
- Huh?

280
00:09:37,625 --> 00:09:38,509
- Yes, carelessness.

281
00:09:38,509 --> 00:09:40,242
- Oh, it just didn't look right.

282
00:09:40,242 --> 00:09:41,409
- Poor speech.

283
00:09:43,726 --> 00:09:45,428
If this speaker would concentrate

284
00:09:45,428 --> 00:09:49,462
upon overcoming his speech
fault of saying uh and ur,

285
00:09:49,462 --> 00:09:52,465
he could be an interesting
and effective speaker.

286
00:09:52,465 --> 00:09:55,699
Does carelessness make
you this type of speaker?

287
00:09:55,699 --> 00:09:56,962
- Think about it, won't you?

288
00:09:56,962 --> 00:09:58,066
Thank you.

289
00:09:58,066 --> 00:10:00,734
- [Professor] Now, this girl
is marked by carelessness-

290
00:10:00,734 --> 00:10:01,567
- And the devil.

291
00:10:01,567 --> 00:10:02,988
- [Professor] Because
she speeds over the words

292
00:10:02,988 --> 00:10:06,174
of her speech just to get them said,

293
00:10:06,174 --> 00:10:09,244
and she drops her voice
at the end of sentences.

294
00:10:09,244 --> 00:10:10,685
- And she drops to the floor

295
00:10:10,685 --> 00:10:14,194
and rolls her eyes into
the back of her head.

296
00:10:14,194 --> 00:10:15,647
- [Professor] Is carelessness making you

297
00:10:15,647 --> 00:10:17,349
this type of speaker?

298
00:10:17,349 --> 00:10:18,499
- Think about it, won't you?

299
00:10:18,499 --> 00:10:19,364
Thank you.

300
00:10:19,364 --> 00:10:21,857
- [Professor] Or is your
carelessness the same as that

301
00:10:21,857 --> 00:10:24,550
of the man who runs his words together?

302
00:10:24,550 --> 00:10:26,908
Mumbles, drops his Gs.

303
00:10:26,908 --> 00:10:31,343
- Drops his pants and
robs convenience stores.

304
00:10:31,343 --> 00:10:33,690
- Have you found yourself yet?

305
00:10:33,690 --> 00:10:36,774
Do you know what your
careless speech habits are?

306
00:10:36,774 --> 00:10:39,475
The important thing is
to recognize the trouble

307
00:10:39,475 --> 00:10:41,567
and then take steps to correct it.

308
00:10:41,567 --> 00:10:43,211
- And then crush it like a cockroach.

309
00:10:43,211 --> 00:10:45,407
- Now let's see if we can help you.

310
00:10:45,407 --> 00:10:48,111
- Don't open beer bottles with your teeth.

311
00:10:48,111 --> 00:10:49,378
- Should practice opening the mouth

312
00:10:49,378 --> 00:10:52,230
wider than usual as he or she speaks.

313
00:10:52,230 --> 00:10:53,307
- And fresh breath, please.

314
00:10:53,307 --> 00:10:54,183
- [Professor] In this way,

315
00:10:54,183 --> 00:10:55,839
the words can flow out,

316
00:10:55,839 --> 00:10:59,657
and the audience can easily hear you.

317
00:10:59,657 --> 00:11:02,751
Speak with life and animation,

318
00:11:02,751 --> 00:11:04,901
yet speak deliberately.

319
00:11:04,901 --> 00:11:07,140
Use plenty of lip and tongue actions.

320
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:08,166
(laughing)

321
00:11:08,166 --> 00:11:09,765
- [Professor] So you
can annunciate clearly,

322
00:11:09,765 --> 00:11:11,932
so you will be understood.

323
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:14,067
- And don't forget the wire rack.

324
00:11:14,067 --> 00:11:16,700
- [Professor] Speak in low full tones,

325
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:18,680
so that what you say will be pleasing

326
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:20,897
and appealing to the ear.

327
00:11:20,897 --> 00:11:21,730
- And potent.

328
00:11:21,730 --> 00:11:23,887
- [Professor] Don't let
your voice be high, shrill,

329
00:11:23,887 --> 00:11:28,202
and unpleasant just because
you're a little excited.

330
00:11:28,202 --> 00:11:30,640
Keep it low and pleasing.

331
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:32,375
- I like it that way.

332
00:11:32,375 --> 00:11:34,031
- Well, here's young George Patton,

333
00:11:34,031 --> 00:11:36,068
a patriot and into high grade weed.

334
00:11:36,068 --> 00:11:37,724
- [Professor] So that each
sentence doesn't sound

335
00:11:37,724 --> 00:11:39,067
just like the other.

336
00:11:39,067 --> 00:11:40,103
- When David Duke is elected,

337
00:11:40,103 --> 00:11:42,541
we will take over the country.

338
00:11:42,541 --> 00:11:43,520
- [Professor] Manner of speaking must

339
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:45,737
be pleasant and interesting.

340
00:11:45,737 --> 00:11:46,865
- Do I please you?

341
00:11:46,865 --> 00:11:48,015
- And finally, let your voice

342
00:11:48,015 --> 00:11:51,819
and speaking manner reflect
your own personality.

343
00:11:51,819 --> 00:11:54,210
Let it be sincere and typical of you,

344
00:11:54,210 --> 00:11:57,399
rather than stilted and stagey.

345
00:11:57,399 --> 00:11:58,232
- Like me.

346
00:11:58,232 --> 00:11:59,101
(chuckling)

347
00:11:59,101 --> 00:12:03,109
- Ladies, I'm so glad to see you all here,

348
00:12:03,109 --> 00:12:05,094
and see you looking so well.

349
00:12:05,094 --> 00:12:06,244
- It's an Aunt Bea convention.

350
00:12:06,244 --> 00:12:10,034
- Our subject this afternoon is flowers.

351
00:12:10,034 --> 00:12:12,451
Don't you just adore flowers?

352
00:12:13,353 --> 00:12:14,786
- She is one hot mama.

353
00:12:14,786 --> 00:12:16,626
- Do you see what I mean?

354
00:12:16,626 --> 00:12:19,432
Now what she really meant to say was this.

355
00:12:19,432 --> 00:12:20,837
- My life is a hollow lie.

356
00:12:20,837 --> 00:12:22,756
- That you've come out today, ladies.

357
00:12:22,756 --> 00:12:25,032
Glad to see you looking so well.

358
00:12:25,032 --> 00:12:27,390
The subject of our discussion is flowers,

359
00:12:27,390 --> 00:12:30,208
one of which I'm particularly fond.

360
00:12:30,208 --> 00:12:31,876
- Poppies.
- I know that many of you too

361
00:12:31,876 --> 00:12:34,690
share my great enthusiasm
for this subject.

362
00:12:34,690 --> 00:12:37,890
- The idea is to be interesting.

363
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:39,672
Interesting, but real.

364
00:12:39,672 --> 00:12:41,707
- Firm yet yielding to the touch.

365
00:12:41,707 --> 00:12:43,582
- Now are there any questions?
- Professor Buehler?

366
00:12:43,582 --> 00:12:44,941
- [Professor] Yes?

367
00:12:44,941 --> 00:12:46,597
- Won't people think I'm peculiar

368
00:12:46,597 --> 00:12:49,863
if I start speaking differently
than I ever have before?

369
00:12:49,863 --> 00:12:50,696
- People think that already.

370
00:12:50,696 --> 00:12:52,715
- Now that's a good point,

371
00:12:52,715 --> 00:12:54,932
but I don't think you need worry about it.

372
00:12:54,932 --> 00:12:58,189
You see, your speaking voice
will become more adequate.

373
00:12:58,189 --> 00:12:59,799
- Sounds like Brenda Vacarro.

374
00:12:59,799 --> 00:13:01,179
- Just the rules of good speaking.

375
00:13:01,179 --> 00:13:03,788
And your friends won't
think that you're peculiar.

376
00:13:03,788 --> 00:13:04,867
On the other hand,

377
00:13:04,867 --> 00:13:06,662
they're more likely to admire you.

378
00:13:06,662 --> 00:13:08,480
- For having the courage
to come out in public.

379
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:10,952
- having improved your manner of speaking.

380
00:13:10,952 --> 00:13:13,770
Speak audibly so you may be heard.

381
00:13:13,770 --> 00:13:15,217
- John Heard.

382
00:13:15,217 --> 00:13:18,462
- Speak distinctly so
you may be understood.

383
00:13:18,462 --> 00:13:19,714
- [All] Huh?

384
00:13:19,714 --> 00:13:21,612
- And speak with life and enthusiasm,

385
00:13:21,612 --> 00:13:23,936
so you may be pleasing.

386
00:13:23,936 --> 00:13:26,203
- And get yourself a good wire rack.

387
00:13:26,203 --> 00:13:28,708
- And remember that good speech will be

388
00:13:28,708 --> 00:13:30,904
a great asset to you always.

389
00:13:30,904 --> 00:13:33,710
It is the mark of a cultured person

390
00:13:33,710 --> 00:13:35,817
and a well-developed personality.

391
00:13:35,817 --> 00:13:36,976
- And a good friend.

392
00:13:36,976 --> 00:13:41,852
- Take pride in your voice
and your speaking manner.

393
00:13:41,852 --> 00:13:43,921
Take pride in the way you sound.

394
00:13:43,921 --> 00:13:45,084
- Put out some chips or something.

395
00:13:45,084 --> 00:13:46,544
Didn't your mother teach you anything?

396
00:13:46,544 --> 00:13:49,601
- Be careful how you use it.

397
00:13:49,601 --> 00:13:51,674
(patriotic music)

398
00:13:51,674 --> 00:13:54,270
- Look, there's a hammer and
a sickle in that eye, beware!

399
00:13:54,270 --> 00:13:56,814
- Yay, speech, speech!

400
00:13:56,814 --> 00:13:59,001
Speech, woo, speech!
(whistling)

401
00:13:59,001 --> 00:13:59,862
- Speech?

402
00:13:59,862 --> 00:14:01,977
- Speech, lip and tongue action, woo!

403
00:14:01,977 --> 00:14:02,977
Woo, author!

404
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:05,653
(laughing)

405
00:14:05,653 --> 00:14:08,236
(upbeat music)

406
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:14,300
- Say, impressive.

407
00:14:16,492 --> 00:14:18,850
This film's a capitol idea.

408
00:14:18,850 --> 00:14:20,600
The seal of approval.

409
00:14:21,472 --> 00:14:22,723
Well, good night, everybody, got to go,

410
00:14:22,723 --> 00:14:23,556
- Oh.

411
00:14:25,039 --> 00:14:26,357
- Made you look.

412
00:14:26,357 --> 00:14:29,524
- Hey Charlotte, it spells out a word.

413
00:14:32,545 --> 00:14:34,246
- Earth Versus by Walt Whitman.

414
00:14:34,246 --> 00:14:35,649
- No, no, no, no.

415
00:14:35,649 --> 00:14:38,732
- I'm putting my money on the spider.

416
00:14:43,837 --> 00:14:46,623
- Hey, this film's
slipping us a Mickey, what?

417
00:14:46,623 --> 00:14:48,873
- Rather be slipped a Finn.

418
00:14:49,854 --> 00:14:50,687
- No.

419
00:14:52,771 --> 00:14:54,704
- Hey, do you bet that
Jack Webb's in this?

420
00:14:54,704 --> 00:14:56,204
- Oh, that's cute.

421
00:14:58,062 --> 00:15:01,085
- This spider keeps seeing the credits.

422
00:15:01,085 --> 00:15:04,252
- Shirley Falls and it's not my fault.

423
00:15:05,087 --> 00:15:06,754
It's Earl Pelbert's.

424
00:15:09,022 --> 00:15:11,401
- Hey, a rolling stone
gathers no Marty Moss.

425
00:15:11,401 --> 00:15:12,734
- I suppose not.

426
00:15:13,772 --> 00:15:14,830
- It's just a credit line.

427
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:17,047
- Hey, Paul Michael's father.

428
00:15:17,047 --> 00:15:18,657
- That's Glaser, Glaser.

429
00:15:18,657 --> 00:15:19,490
- Oh.

430
00:15:20,326 --> 00:15:21,960
- Oh, assistant technical effects?

431
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:24,018
Oh, honey, hand me that Tonka truck.

432
00:15:24,018 --> 00:15:25,327
- What a burn.

433
00:15:25,327 --> 00:15:26,558
- Gorog write screenplay.

434
00:15:26,558 --> 00:15:30,308
(primeval grunting)

435
00:15:30,308 --> 00:15:32,746
- No spiders were squished,
stepped on, flushed,

436
00:15:32,746 --> 00:15:34,736
or made to suffer any emotional distress

437
00:15:34,736 --> 00:15:36,162
during the making of this film.

438
00:15:36,162 --> 00:15:37,771
One spider did die of old age.

439
00:15:37,771 --> 00:15:42,187
We have two letters from
doctors confirming this.

440
00:15:42,187 --> 00:15:43,270
- Scared yet?

441
00:15:44,739 --> 00:15:45,572
- I'm shaking.

442
00:15:45,572 --> 00:15:47,239
I'm shaking, people.

443
00:15:48,294 --> 00:15:49,628
They're not very proud of these people.

444
00:15:49,628 --> 00:15:51,128
- No one would be.

445
00:15:53,433 --> 00:15:56,390
- Our spider seems to
be shorting out, sir.

446
00:15:56,390 --> 00:15:57,724
- It's blocking the union seal there.

447
00:15:57,724 --> 00:15:58,723
- It's getting too close.

448
00:15:58,723 --> 00:16:03,176
- Come into my parlor said
the spider to the titles.

449
00:16:03,176 --> 00:16:05,509
- Spider by Spencer's Gifts.

450
00:16:08,392 --> 00:16:09,809
- Anytown, U.S.A.

451
00:16:11,545 --> 00:16:12,378
- Good place.

452
00:16:12,378 --> 00:16:13,799
- Hey, you're driving on the sidewalk.

453
00:16:13,799 --> 00:16:14,966
Hey, hey, hey!

454
00:16:16,789 --> 00:16:18,372
- I'm liquid metal.

455
00:16:19,264 --> 00:16:20,849
- Let me tell you a
little bit about myself.

456
00:16:20,849 --> 00:16:21,682
I drive a truck,

457
00:16:21,682 --> 00:16:22,515
I'm butt ugly,

458
00:16:22,515 --> 00:16:24,098
and I hate spiders.

459
00:16:28,786 --> 00:16:30,626
My lips are big.

460
00:16:30,626 --> 00:16:31,739
- That dad of mine.

461
00:16:31,739 --> 00:16:32,638
He's so thoughtful,

462
00:16:32,638 --> 00:16:34,305
and a nightgown too.

463
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:40,745
(screaming)

464
00:16:40,745 --> 00:16:42,595
- Hey, he filled the light with.

465
00:16:42,595 --> 00:16:46,450
- I'm guessing the film is not about him.

466
00:16:46,450 --> 00:16:47,542
- We're sorry that had to happen,

467
00:16:47,542 --> 00:16:51,878
now let's get back to our
story in Cleantown, USA.

468
00:16:51,878 --> 00:16:53,589
- Why don't I feel fresh?
- Carol?

469
00:16:53,589 --> 00:16:54,901
- Hey Carol, wait.

470
00:16:54,901 --> 00:16:56,695
- Hi, Goonie.
- Hi, Michael.

471
00:16:56,695 --> 00:16:57,812
- Did you hear about your dad?

472
00:16:57,812 --> 00:16:58,645
- Hey, wake up.

473
00:16:58,645 --> 00:17:00,204
This is Mike, remember?

474
00:17:00,204 --> 00:17:01,854
The boy with the golden heart
and the memory of an elephant.

475
00:17:01,854 --> 00:17:04,344
- And the teeth of a chipmunk.

476
00:17:04,344 --> 00:17:05,770
- Look, I remembered, see?

477
00:17:05,770 --> 00:17:07,354
- Something to remember your dad by.

478
00:17:07,354 --> 00:17:08,748
- Happy birthday, Carol.

479
00:17:08,748 --> 00:17:09,581
- Thanks.

480
00:17:13,507 --> 00:17:14,599
- Aren't you going to open it?

481
00:17:14,599 --> 00:17:17,462
- I'll have to return it first.

482
00:17:17,462 --> 00:17:19,199
- What's wrong?

483
00:17:19,199 --> 00:17:20,833
- It's Dad.

484
00:17:20,833 --> 00:17:22,005
He didn't come home last night.

485
00:17:22,005 --> 00:17:22,838
- So what?

486
00:17:22,838 --> 00:17:24,029
You know it doesn't mean a thing.

487
00:17:24,029 --> 00:17:26,236
- If it ain't got that swing.
- You know how he is.

488
00:17:26,236 --> 00:17:27,866
- Yeah, he's a boozy drunkard.

489
00:17:27,866 --> 00:17:28,699
- Oh!

490
00:17:28,699 --> 00:17:30,309
- What do you mean I know how he is?

491
00:17:30,309 --> 00:17:31,723
- Slap him.

492
00:17:31,723 --> 00:17:32,556
- Nothing.

493
00:17:37,172 --> 00:17:38,414
(horn honking)

494
00:17:38,414 --> 00:17:39,773
- Ah, just annoying my girlfriend.

495
00:17:39,773 --> 00:17:41,523
I'm sorry, excuse me.

496
00:17:46,293 --> 00:17:47,126
- Oh, you're right.

497
00:17:47,126 --> 00:17:48,959
My dad's gutter swill.

498
00:17:50,563 --> 00:17:53,099
- Well he went to Springdale
yesterday to buy me a present.

499
00:17:53,099 --> 00:17:55,285
He said he'd be back
before dinner last night,

500
00:17:55,285 --> 00:17:57,115
but he still hadn't come back yet.

501
00:17:57,115 --> 00:17:58,403
- That doesn't mean a thing, Carol.

502
00:17:58,403 --> 00:18:00,071
I mean, it isn't the first time.

503
00:18:00,071 --> 00:18:01,643
- Doh!

504
00:18:01,643 --> 00:18:02,896
- You're mean, Mike.

505
00:18:02,896 --> 00:18:04,015
- That's why I love you.

506
00:18:04,015 --> 00:18:05,404
- I don't want your silly present either.

507
00:18:05,404 --> 00:18:06,237
Here!

508
00:18:07,452 --> 00:18:10,035
♪ Dobey, Dobey ♪

509
00:18:12,755 --> 00:18:13,838
- That Dobey.

510
00:18:14,975 --> 00:18:17,815
- No, teacher, no, I'm
sorry I talked in class!

511
00:18:17,815 --> 00:18:19,943
(imitating electricity zapping)

512
00:18:19,943 --> 00:18:20,977
- Okay, who's next?

513
00:18:20,977 --> 00:18:22,850
- Electrons jump from one
electrode to the other

514
00:18:22,850 --> 00:18:25,114
because of the difference
in the electrical potential

515
00:18:25,114 --> 00:18:27,165
between the two poles.
(snoring)

516
00:18:27,165 --> 00:18:28,889
Now this one is the negative pole,

517
00:18:28,889 --> 00:18:32,065
on which electricity flows
to the positive pole.

518
00:18:32,065 --> 00:18:33,687
We call the positive pole what?

519
00:18:33,687 --> 00:18:35,101
- Les qualensza.

520
00:18:35,101 --> 00:18:35,934
- Correct.

521
00:18:35,934 --> 00:18:36,767
The negative pole?

522
00:18:36,767 --> 00:18:37,600
- [All] The axle.

523
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:39,253
- Now what don't I know my butt from?

524
00:18:39,253 --> 00:18:40,149
- [Both] A hole in the ground.

525
00:18:40,149 --> 00:18:41,001
- [Kingman] These electrodes is increased

526
00:18:41,001 --> 00:18:42,931
to a greater amplitude,

527
00:18:42,931 --> 00:18:45,470
- I'm submissive in a '50s kind of way.

528
00:18:45,470 --> 00:18:46,542
- [Kingman] The larger the arc becomes,

529
00:18:46,542 --> 00:18:47,542
the more dangerous it is

530
00:18:47,542 --> 00:18:50,762
to anything that may
be placed in its path.

531
00:18:50,762 --> 00:18:52,832
One thing to remember is
that the flow of electrons

532
00:18:52,832 --> 00:18:55,353
is always from the
