00:27:20,833 --> 00:27:21,666
Mike?

797
00:27:21,666 --> 00:27:22,522
- What?

798
00:27:22,522 --> 00:27:24,283
- You won't go very far will you?

799
00:27:24,283 --> 00:27:26,156
People have gone in there
and never come out again.

800
00:27:26,156 --> 00:27:29,205
- No, no, that was Injun Joe's cave, dear.

801
00:27:29,205 --> 00:27:30,112
Remember?

802
00:27:30,112 --> 00:27:31,945
- Ah, Mr. Carol's Dad?

803
00:27:33,219 --> 00:27:34,850
Mr. Carol's Dad?

804
00:27:34,850 --> 00:27:35,683
- Mike?

805
00:27:38,334 --> 00:27:39,167
Mike?

806
00:27:39,167 --> 00:27:40,417
- I'm doing it.

807
00:27:42,544 --> 00:27:44,187
- I thought I told you to stay outside.

808
00:27:44,187 --> 00:27:45,416
Now what's the idea of following me?

809
00:27:45,416 --> 00:27:46,333
- I forgot.

810
00:27:48,040 --> 00:27:50,319
- Mike, I'm scared of being alone.

811
00:27:50,319 --> 00:27:52,079
- Oh, aren't we all.

812
00:27:52,079 --> 00:27:53,781
- Do you see any sign of him?

813
00:27:53,781 --> 00:27:55,425
- It wouldn't be right
here near the opening

814
00:27:55,425 --> 00:27:57,592
if he wanted to keep warm.

815
00:27:58,666 --> 00:27:59,841
I'm going to go a little further in.

816
00:27:59,841 --> 00:28:01,591
- I'm going with you.

817
00:28:03,394 --> 00:28:05,039
- Okay.

818
00:28:05,039 --> 00:28:07,075
- Oh, I broke a heel. Carry me.

819
00:28:07,075 --> 00:28:10,156
- It's Carol's dad's cavern.

820
00:28:10,156 --> 00:28:12,999
- Nice, they walked to Arizona.

821
00:28:12,999 --> 00:28:13,832
Cool.

822
00:28:15,503 --> 00:28:18,092
- This looks like the
hideout of Mr. Freeze.

823
00:28:18,092 --> 00:28:18,925
- It's no use.

824
00:28:18,925 --> 00:28:20,543
He doesn't seem to be in here either.

825
00:28:20,543 --> 00:28:22,724
- Oh, let's not give up yet please, Mike.

826
00:28:22,724 --> 00:28:24,002
- Who said anything about giving up?

827
00:28:24,002 --> 00:28:25,235
We're just getting started.

828
00:28:25,235 --> 00:28:26,210
- Yeah, we'll give up in a minute.

829
00:28:26,210 --> 00:28:27,475
- This cave is supposed to go way back

830
00:28:27,475 --> 00:28:29,808
under that mountain forever.

831
00:28:33,880 --> 00:28:34,713
- Dad!

832
00:28:36,792 --> 00:28:37,625
Dad!

833
00:28:41,668 --> 00:28:43,025
- Batman loves Robin.

834
00:28:43,025 --> 00:28:44,014
Penguin bites.

835
00:28:44,014 --> 00:28:45,600
I can't read the rest of it.

836
00:28:45,600 --> 00:28:46,517
- Dad! Dad!

837
00:28:48,304 --> 00:28:50,972
- Boy, your dad sure picked
a great cave to die in.

838
00:28:50,972 --> 00:28:51,805
- Dad!

839
00:28:53,056 --> 00:28:53,889
Dad!

840
00:28:55,664 --> 00:28:59,314
- Hm, I didn't know
they piled it that high.

841
00:28:59,314 --> 00:29:00,314
- Ooh, echo!

842
00:29:06,503 --> 00:29:08,798
(imitating wind whistling)

843
00:29:08,798 --> 00:29:12,378
- How will we ever find
him in a place this big?

844
00:29:12,378 --> 00:29:13,719
- Get a map?

845
00:29:13,719 --> 00:29:15,607
- They're in somebody's tonsils.

846
00:29:15,607 --> 00:29:18,426
- Mike, let's go this way.

847
00:29:18,426 --> 00:29:21,009
- Oh, suddenly you're Magellan?

848
00:29:25,246 --> 00:29:26,079
- Dad!

849
00:29:27,499 --> 00:29:28,812
Dad!

850
00:29:28,812 --> 00:29:29,812
- Beautiful.

851
00:29:31,467 --> 00:29:34,687
- For my next number I'll sing Mom, Mom.

852
00:29:34,687 --> 00:29:35,520
- Uh- oh.

853
00:29:37,195 --> 00:29:38,401
- Dad!

854
00:29:38,401 --> 00:29:39,651
- There you go.

855
00:29:40,830 --> 00:29:43,774
- It looks like Dad had it in for her.

856
00:29:43,774 --> 00:29:45,357
It's a Popsicle, kind of.

857
00:29:45,357 --> 00:29:46,190
Get it?

858
00:29:46,190 --> 00:29:49,716
- Did your dad look like this, Carol?

859
00:29:49,716 --> 00:29:51,799
- Yep, it's a big pork link sausage.

860
00:29:51,799 --> 00:29:53,710
Jimmy Dean tried to kill you.

861
00:29:53,710 --> 00:29:55,421
- How do they make that pure pork sausage?

862
00:29:55,421 --> 00:29:58,754
- Well, they take some pork-
- Don't ask.

863
00:30:01,703 --> 00:30:03,956
- Oh, go ahead, I'll be back here.

864
00:30:03,956 --> 00:30:08,123
Oh, you might want to stop
shrieking for your dad.

865
00:30:09,132 --> 00:30:10,382
- Nice back up.

866
00:30:13,870 --> 00:30:14,960
- This is a really neat date.

867
00:30:14,960 --> 00:30:18,960
We're having an adventure
just like the Goonies.

868
00:30:21,687 --> 00:30:22,665
- Dad?

869
00:30:22,665 --> 00:30:23,748
- What? Oh, sorry.

870
00:30:23,748 --> 00:30:25,415
Just kidding, Carol.

871
00:30:26,514 --> 00:30:27,668
(screaming)

872
00:30:27,668 --> 00:30:28,501
- Hi, Carol.

873
00:30:28,501 --> 00:30:29,889
Did you get my present?

874
00:30:29,889 --> 00:30:31,750
- They're in Michael Jackson's basement.

875
00:30:31,750 --> 00:30:33,397
- We've got to go back.

876
00:30:33,397 --> 00:30:35,350
- I think you're right.

877
00:30:35,350 --> 00:30:36,764
- But I can't go without-

878
00:30:36,764 --> 00:30:40,318
- Checking to see if there's
any meat left on them.

879
00:30:40,318 --> 00:30:44,568
- You might as well look
a little further, I guess.

880
00:30:47,185 --> 00:30:48,816
- Hey, do you guys got
a sandwich or something?

881
00:30:48,816 --> 00:30:49,899
I'm famished.

882
00:30:52,199 --> 00:30:53,322
- You remember what I always say?

883
00:30:53,322 --> 00:30:55,222
You can never be too rich or too thin.

884
00:30:55,222 --> 00:30:56,055
(laughing)

885
00:30:56,055 --> 00:30:58,305
That's one of my favorites.

886
00:31:02,168 --> 00:31:03,001
- Don't look now.

887
00:31:03,001 --> 00:31:04,190
- The anxious edge.

888
00:31:04,190 --> 00:31:06,057
- Mike, I can't see.

889
00:31:06,057 --> 00:31:07,425
(screaming)

890
00:31:07,425 --> 00:31:10,737
- How come we can see and you can't?

891
00:31:10,737 --> 00:31:13,530
- Oh, this is the cave
of the Flying Wallendas.

892
00:31:13,530 --> 00:31:14,968
- Are you okay, Carol?

893
00:31:14,968 --> 00:31:16,051
- I think so.

894
00:31:18,615 --> 00:31:20,294
- It's a Sealy Posturepedic.

895
00:31:20,294 --> 00:31:23,044
I just ripped off the tag though.

896
00:31:23,936 --> 00:31:25,186
- I don't know.

897
00:31:26,586 --> 00:31:28,538
Awfully sticky though.

898
00:31:28,538 --> 00:31:30,830
How do we get loose?

899
00:31:30,830 --> 00:31:33,427
- Have a little wine, maybe?

900
00:31:33,427 --> 00:31:34,624
- It's just like glue.

901
00:31:34,624 --> 00:31:37,442
- Oh great, I can finish my
color Ranchero car model.

902
00:31:37,442 --> 00:31:39,018
Cool, oh, wow, with slicks-

903
00:31:39,018 --> 00:31:41,524
- Ropey stuff we found up on the road.

904
00:31:41,524 --> 00:31:43,156
(eerie screeching)

905
00:31:43,156 --> 00:31:44,032
- What's that noise?

906
00:31:44,032 --> 00:31:44,865
- I don't know.

907
00:31:44,865 --> 00:31:47,458
Maybe something unspeakably horrible.

908
00:31:47,458 --> 00:31:50,322
Oh, that's just a theremin.

909
00:31:50,322 --> 00:31:51,155
No worry.

910
00:31:52,392 --> 00:31:55,624
- It's a regular net
we're on, you know that?

911
00:31:55,624 --> 00:31:57,278
- As compared to what?

912
00:31:57,278 --> 00:31:58,532
- An irregular net?

913
00:31:58,532 --> 00:31:59,365
- I suppose.

914
00:31:59,365 --> 00:32:00,336
- Wholesale?

915
00:32:00,336 --> 00:32:01,604
(eerie screeching)

916
00:32:01,604 --> 00:32:03,271
- There it is again.

917
00:32:04,962 --> 00:32:08,182
- Sounds like someone
or something has asthma.

918
00:32:08,182 --> 00:32:09,572
- Yeah, get them a Primatene Mist.

919
00:32:09,572 --> 00:32:12,722
(screaming)

920
00:32:12,722 --> 00:32:14,954
- Oh, come on, it's only
a process shot, honey.

921
00:32:14,954 --> 00:32:17,787
Don't worry, it's nowhere near us.

922
00:32:19,047 --> 00:32:20,728
(screaming)

923
00:32:20,728 --> 00:32:22,108
Oh, well see you, Carol.

924
00:32:22,108 --> 00:32:23,108
I got to go.

925
00:32:24,141 --> 00:32:28,391
- Hey, hey, hey, there's
a time and place for that.

926
00:32:29,247 --> 00:32:31,080
- Now is not the time.

927
00:32:34,445 --> 00:32:35,278
- Okay, wait a second,

928
00:32:35,278 --> 00:32:36,944
wait a second. I gotta,
Mike and Carol were here.

929
00:32:36,944 --> 00:32:38,999
Okay, let's go.

930
00:32:38,999 --> 00:32:39,840
- Hey, come back.

931
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:41,474
I'm actually beneficial.

932
00:32:41,474 --> 00:32:43,461
I eat harmful household pests.

933
00:32:43,461 --> 00:32:45,794
Jim Morrison drank my venom.

934
00:32:47,165 --> 00:32:48,221
- Household pets?

935
00:32:48,221 --> 00:32:49,054
- Great effect.

936
00:32:49,054 --> 00:32:50,604
He's climbing on a postcard.

937
00:32:50,604 --> 00:32:51,437
- Oh.

938
00:32:53,928 --> 00:32:56,011
- Did your dad like bran?

939
00:32:57,528 --> 00:32:59,353
- I can't say for sure
without having it analyzed,

940
00:32:59,353 --> 00:33:03,462
but it does look like a
large piece of natural silk.

941
00:33:03,462 --> 00:33:05,401
- What did we tell you?

942
00:33:05,401 --> 00:33:07,912
- But that doesn't necessarily
prove there's a giant spider.

943
00:33:07,912 --> 00:33:09,672
That part's pretty hard to swallow.

944
00:33:09,672 --> 00:33:10,505
- But we saw it!

945
00:33:10,505 --> 00:33:11,498
- I know a lady who swallowed a spider.

946
00:33:11,498 --> 00:33:14,235
- Maybe my son is not in the
habit of lying, Mrs. Kingman.

947
00:33:14,235 --> 00:33:15,179
- That isn't what Helen meant.

948
00:33:15,179 --> 00:33:16,012
- Not much.

949
00:33:16,012 --> 00:33:17,571
- Whether we believe their story or not,

950
00:33:17,571 --> 00:33:19,616
a man to see about it is the sheriff.

951
00:33:19,616 --> 00:33:21,239
- But we didn't see the deputy.

952
00:33:21,239 --> 00:33:22,146
- Yeah, sure, see the sheriff.

953
00:33:22,146 --> 00:33:22,979
We did.

954
00:33:23,965 --> 00:33:25,631
- He didn't believe them.

955
00:33:25,631 --> 00:33:26,464
- Okay.

956
00:33:30,542 --> 00:33:33,959
- That's it, I'm calling Pants World now.

957
00:33:36,106 --> 00:33:38,719
(telephone ringing)

958
00:33:38,719 --> 00:33:41,629
- Hey, it's Alan Hale and Stuart Pankin.

959
00:33:41,629 --> 00:33:43,879
(laughing)

960
00:33:45,597 --> 00:33:47,667
- Hello, rock-stupid cops.

961
00:33:47,667 --> 00:33:49,093
- Oh yes, Mr. Kingman.

962
00:33:49,093 --> 00:33:51,194
What can I do for you?

963
00:33:51,194 --> 00:33:53,163
Don't go yet, it's only
a high school teacher.

964
00:33:53,163 --> 00:33:55,138
- One more game.

965
00:33:55,138 --> 00:33:57,293
- Well, you know these
teenagers as well as I do,

966
00:33:57,293 --> 00:33:58,949
Mr. Kingman.

967
00:33:58,949 --> 00:34:00,581
Yeah, what will they think of next?

968
00:34:00,581 --> 00:34:02,215
- They're goofballs, you know?

969
00:34:02,215 --> 00:34:03,048
(laughing)

970
00:34:03,048 --> 00:34:04,101
- Spiders?

971
00:34:04,101 --> 00:34:05,159
I told them to bring me one,

972
00:34:05,159 --> 00:34:06,502
and I'd believe it.

973
00:34:06,502 --> 00:34:08,459
- Well, I can't say I blame you, Sheriff.

974
00:34:08,459 --> 00:34:10,633
Naturally, I didn't call you
up to get you to investigate

975
00:34:10,633 --> 00:34:12,565
abnormal insect life,

976
00:34:12,565 --> 00:34:13,875
but Flynn is still missing.
- I love him.

977
00:34:13,875 --> 00:34:16,107
The officers found
traces of him out there.

978
00:34:16,107 --> 00:34:17,463
- What are they?

979
00:34:17,463 --> 00:34:19,653
- You may be doing yourself a
favor if you looked into that.

980
00:34:19,653 --> 00:34:20,930
- Well, you took the words
right out of my mouth,

981
00:34:20,930 --> 00:34:21,850
Mr. Kingman.

982
00:34:21,850 --> 00:34:23,920
- And he took a doughnut
out of my mouth. (laughing)

983
00:34:23,920 --> 00:34:25,082
It's just one more.

984
00:34:25,082 --> 00:34:26,162
- I love those.

985
00:34:26,162 --> 00:34:28,072
- We're rounding up a
search party right now.

986
00:34:28,072 --> 00:34:28,980
Get half a dozen men.

987
00:34:28,980 --> 00:34:30,682
Spencer, Haskell, maybe old Jake.

988
00:34:30,682 --> 00:34:31,866
Anyone else that isn't busy.

989
00:34:31,866 --> 00:34:35,062
- Biscuits, coleslaw,
just get the Meal Maker.

990
00:34:35,062 --> 00:34:35,895
- See?

991
00:34:38,594 --> 00:34:40,216
- Well, I'll need those two kids with us

992
00:34:40,216 --> 00:34:41,618
to point things out,

993
00:34:41,618 --> 00:34:44,504
and if you want to come
along too, that's fine.

994
00:34:44,504 --> 00:34:46,288
- You know, I think
that's Gary Busey's dad.

995
00:34:46,288 --> 00:34:47,264
- Don't you worry,

996
00:34:47,264 --> 00:34:48,288
we'll bring rifles-

997
00:34:48,288 --> 00:34:49,886
- Maybe because Gary Busey.

998
00:34:49,886 --> 00:34:51,118
- Run into that spider.

999
00:34:51,118 --> 00:34:53,368
(laughing)

1000
00:34:55,615 --> 00:34:56,876
Anything else on your mind?

1001
00:34:56,876 --> 00:34:57,709
- Well, speaking of spiders,

1002
00:34:57,709 --> 00:34:59,406
are you sure rifles are just the thing?

1003
00:34:59,406 --> 00:35:02,640
Insects have a pretty simple
nervous system, Sheriff.

1004
00:35:02,640 --> 00:35:03,698
You could plug holes in one all day

1005
00:35:03,698 --> 00:35:04,998
and never hit a vital spot.

1006
00:35:04,998 --> 00:35:06,090
- Oh, it's fun.

1007
00:35:06,090 --> 00:35:07,031
- You want to be on the safe side,

1008
00:35:07,031 --> 00:35:09,322
call the pest control
people in Springdale.

1009
00:35:09,322 --> 00:35:11,666
Have them send out all
the DDT they can find.

1010
00:35:11,666 --> 00:35:12,499
- Good.

1011
00:35:13,760 --> 00:35:16,438
(laughing)

1012
00:35:16,438 --> 00:35:18,438
Giant spider, what next?

1013
00:35:21,258 --> 00:35:23,429
- So I was sitting by
myself and I said, "Spider."

1014
00:35:23,429 --> 00:35:24,262
(laughing)

1015
00:35:24,262 --> 00:35:25,891
I crack me up.

1016
00:35:25,891 --> 00:35:27,974
- You smell like chicken.

1017
00:35:29,348 --> 00:35:31,348
- What the hell is that?

1018
00:35:32,864 --> 00:35:34,980
- Now bring your bug juice and let's go.

1019
00:35:34,980 --> 00:35:36,302
- What are we using it at?

1020
00:35:36,302 --> 00:35:37,565
- Does it make any difference?

1021
00:35:37,565 --> 00:35:38,945
- Well, I've got to mix the DDT according

1022
00:35:38,945 --> 00:35:40,693
to what you want killed.

1023
00:35:40,693 --> 00:35:42,591
- What will I tell him, Professor?

1024
00:35:42,591 --> 00:35:43,431
Spiders?

1025
00:35:43,431 --> 00:35:44,707
(laughing)

1026
00:35:44,707 --> 00:35:46,802
- Is the usual dose.

1027
00:35:46,802 --> 00:35:48,596
- Make it 50.

1028
00:35:48,596 --> 00:35:49,939
- I'll have to charge you extra.

1029
00:35:49,939 --> 00:35:52,367
- Hey, they were a double
golden slasher time, guys.

1030
00:35:52,367 --> 00:35:53,200
He said 50.

1031
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:54,137
Let's do it.

1032
00:35:54,137 --> 00:35:56,720
(upbeat music)

1033
00:36:02,002 --> 00:36:02,919
- Ah, yeah.

1034
00:36:04,730 --> 00:36:06,892
Oops, I slipped on a
little of your dad there.

1035
00:36:06,892 --> 00:36:08,548
I'll have to throw my shoe in the grave.

1036
00:36:08,548 --> 00:36:09,848
(laughing)

1037
00:36:09,848 --> 00:36:11,348
- Oh, that's cute.

1038
00:36:13,043 --> 00:36:13,996
- I suppose you're going to tell me

1039
00:36:13,996 --> 00:36:15,205
this is your dad's truck,

1040
00:36:15,205 --> 00:36:16,493
and the spider did this?

1041
00:36:16,493 --> 00:36:18,149
(laughing)

1042
00:36:18,149 --> 00:36:19,542
- The spider grabbed the truck.

1043
00:36:19,542 --> 00:36:20,863
- Okay, bunny hop, everybody.

1044
00:36:20,863 --> 00:36:22,530
Let's make this fun.

1045
00:36:25,304 --> 00:36:26,534
- I got to squeeze through here.

1046
00:36:26,534 --> 00:36:27,855
Watch our for your dad's head.

1047
00:36:27,855 --> 00:36:29,396
(laughing)

1048
00:36:29,396 --> 00:36:32,296
- Everybody venerate the truck as you go.

1049
00:36:32,296 --> 00:36:33,639
It's a hoot.

1050
00:36:33,639 --> 00:36:35,889
(laughing)

1051
00:36:41,818 --> 00:36:43,235
- Come on, Pokey.

1052
00:36:45,404 --> 00:36:47,936
- Now I suppose you're going
to tell me this is a cave.

1053
00:36:47,936 --> 00:36:49,923
(laughing)

1054
00:36:49,923 --> 00:36:51,257
- Oh, I broke my heel.

1055
00:36:51,257 --> 00:36:52,090
Carry me.

1056
00:36:52,090 --> 00:36:54,340
(laughing)

1057
00:36:55,765 --> 00:36:56,639
- Which way, kids?

1058
00:36:56,639 --> 00:36:58,056
- Straight ahead.

1059
00:37:02,066 --> 00:37:04,174
- Now why did they invite the milkman?

1060
00:37:04,174 --> 00:37:05,818
- Oh, something new was coming to town.

1061
00:37:05,818 --> 00:37:09,406
- You can't tell the
difference with their hats off.

1062
00:37:09,406 --> 00:37:10,614
- It looks like you had this room done

1063
00:37:10,614 --> 00:37:12,368
in early paleolithic here.

1064
00:37:12,368 --> 00:37:15,444
(chuckling)

1065
00:37:15,444 --> 00:37:18,527
- Hey, this music wasn't here before.

1066
00:37:19,939 --> 00:37:22,106
- Elk Master on the scene.

1067
00:37:26,493 --> 00:37:28,575
- Someplace, make a nice Elks hall,

1068
00:37:28,575 --> 00:37:29,408
you know that?

1069
00:37:29,408 --> 00:37:32,209
- Where would the Brewmeister
and the pull tabs go?

1070
00:37:32,209 --> 00:37:34,384
- You think we'll find that
girl's pa in here alive?

1071
00:37:34,384 --> 00:37:35,217
- Hell no.

1072
00:37:35,217 --> 00:37:36,558
Oh sorry.

1073
00:37:36,558 --> 00:37:39,304
- The spiders have a habit of
stunning their prey with venom

1074
00:37:39,304 --> 00:37:40,293
and storing them in a silk bag

1075
00:37:40,293 --> 00:37:42,087
til they're ready to feed on them.

1076
00:37:42,087 --> 00:37:43,203
- Like an Anne Klein?

1077
00:37:43,203 --> 00:37:44,378
- I suppose.

1078
00:37:44,378 --> 00:37:45,859
- Not quite.

1079
00:37:45,859 --> 00:37:46,975
They drain the liquid from the bodies,

1080
00:37:46,975 --> 00:37:48,618
leaving only the skin and bones.

1081
00:37:48,618 --> 00:37:50,319
- So we're looking for a drunk spider?

1082
00:37:50,319 --> 00:37:51,627
- Mind you've got.

1083
00:37:51,627 --> 00:37:53,969
Alright, let's look for Flynn,

1084
00:37:53,969 --> 00:37:55,638
and don't lose sight
of the man next to you.

1085
00:37:55,638 --> 00:37:57,570
No sense in anyone getting lost.

1086
00:37:57,570 --> 00:37:59,431
- Well, look for a dry guy in a silk bag.

1087
00:37:59,431 --> 00:38:00,514
Pass it down.

1088
00:38:03,780 --> 00:38:05,365
- Buddy up and enjoy the scenery.

1089
00:38:05,365 --> 00:38:07,448
No sense in wasting that.

1090
00:38:09,197 --> 00:38:10,310
- Oh wait a minute.

1091
00:38:10,310 --> 00:38:12,242
That says Arne Saknussemm on this wall.

1092
00:38:12,242 --> 00:38:13,610
- This is a waste of time.

1093
00:38:13,610 --> 00:38:15,060
You know something?

1094
00:38:15,060 --> 00:38:16,368
I haven't seen a spider yet,

1095
00:38:16,368 --> 00:38:18,476
and I don't think we will.

1096
00:38:18,476 --> 00:38:20,224
- You will if he's still there.

1097
00:38:20,224 --> 00:38:23,248
- It'd be just dialogue if he
moved into the next county.

1098
00:38:23,248 --> 00:38:24,202
(laughing)

1099
00:38:24,202 --> 00:38:26,177
- I don't think you'll be disappointed.

1100
00:38:26,177 --> 00:38:27,973
There's something in here all right.

1101
00:38:27,973 --> 00:38:29,562
You'd naturally expect
to find a certain amount

1102
00:38:29,562 --> 00:38:31,218
of wildlife in a cave.

1103
00:38:31,218 --> 00:38:32,574
Rats, mice, bats, and so on.

1104
00:38:32,574 --> 00:38:33,942
- The occasional Japanese soldier.

1105
00:38:33,942 --> 00:38:34,942
- You don't.

1106
00:38:36,232 --> 00:38:38,796
They've been frightened out.

1107
00:38:38,796 --> 00:38:39,629
- Ah.

1108
00:38:41,754 --> 00:38:42,837
- I'm Batman.

1109
00:38:45,014 --> 00:38:46,569
(rifle shooting)

1110
00:38:46,569 --> 00:38:49,902
- Oh, now we have to pay Jack Nicholson.

1111
00:38:51,505 --> 00:38:53,232
- No wildlife, eh Kingman?

1112
00:38:53,232 --> 00:38:54,399
- Not anymore.

1113
00:38:55,716 --> 00:38:58,614
- That's about as big
as they come, I'd say.

1114
00:38:58,614 --> 00:39:01,083
- I wouldn't handle it if I
were you. It might have rabies.

1115
00:39:01,083 --> 00:39:02,416
- Or girl germs.

1116
00:39:03,695 --> 00:39:06,195
(eerie music)

1117
00:39:11,540 --> 00:39:15,540
- I know that theremin
is around here somewhere.

1118
00:39:24,235 --> 00:39:25,068
- Are you happy now?

1119
00:39:25,068 --> 00:39:26,068
I buried it.

1120
00:39:27,467 --> 00:39:29,525
- There, it's buried.

1121
00:39:29,525 --> 00:39:31,846
Want to say a few words over it, Sheriff?

1122
00:39:31,846 --> 00:39:32,679
- Oh sure.

1123
00:39:32,679 --> 00:39:36,346
Dear Lord-
(Carol screaming)

1124
00:39:37,201 --> 00:39:39,000
(crying)

1125
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,818
- Oh, the snap's in the front, you dummy.

1126
00:39:41,818 --> 00:39:43,174
- It's her father.

1127
00:39:43,174 --> 00:39:46,257
(music intensifying)

1128
00:39:47,866 --> 00:39:50,038
- Doh, it's Rose Kennedy.

1129
00:39:50,038 --> 00:39:51,121
Looking good.

1130
00:39:54,720 --> 00:39:56,560
- Hey, is that Statler or Waldorf?

1131
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:58,252
- When you get back to town,

1132
00:39:58,252 --> 00:40:00,182
make out a coroner's report.

1133
00:40:00,182 --> 00:40:02,445
Just put down Jack Flynn as the name.

1134
00:40:02,445 --> 00:40:03,346
- Dry's the game.

1135
00:40:03,346 --> 00:40:05,014
- Cause of death?

1136
00:40:05,014 --> 00:40:06,014
- It was a spider.

1137
00:40:06,014 --> 00:40:07,636
It drained all the liquid out of his body.

1138
00:40:07,636 --> 00:40:09,912
- Just put down cause unknown,

1139
00:40:09,912 --> 00:40:11,508
and let the coroner worry about the rest.

1140
00:40:11,508 --> 00:40:12,810
- And put some lotion on him or something.

1141
00:40:12,810 --> 00:40:14,052
He looks like hell.

1142
00:40:14,052 --> 00:40:15,272
- That's the trouble with you egg-heads.

1143
00:40:15,272 --> 00:40:16,962
You jump to conclusions.

1144
00:40:16,962 --> 00:40:17,904
I know what I see,

1145
00:40:17,904 --> 00:40:19,538
and I see a dead man,

1146
00:40:19,538 --> 00:40:23,146
but I don't see any spider.
- Do we pretend to know why,

1147
00:40:23,146 --> 00:40:25,116
but I know what I like.

1148
00:40:25,116 --> 00:40:27,232
- Where was the web?

1149
00:40:27,232 --> 00:40:29,065
- Right through there.

1150
00:40:30,130 --> 00:40:31,474
Wait a minute, you don't
know what you're doing.

1151
00:40:31,474 --> 00:40:33,728
You can't just walk in there.

1152
00:40:33,728 --> 00:40:34,614
- Why not?

1153
00:40:34,614 --> 00:40:35,880
- You think I'm going
to die a horrible death

1154
00:40:35,880 --> 00:40:37,604
like her old man?

1155
00:40:37,604 --> 00:40:38,512
I'm kidding.

1156
00:40:38,512 --> 00:40:41,492
- Were are you close to him?

1157
00:40:41,492 --> 00:40:42,630
- Nah, nonsense.

1158
00:40:42,630 --> 00:40:46,130
Some circus act from out of town did that.

1159
00:40:47,447 --> 00:40:50,792
- Hmm, yeah, oh, oh,
big, oh, no, ooh, ooh.

1160
00:40:50,792 --> 00:40:52,393
- Find any giant spiders, Sheriff?

1161
00:40:52,393 --> 00:40:53,866
- Ah, shut up.

1162
00:40:53,866 --> 00:40:55,715
- Get that DDT in here quick.

1163
00:40:55,715 --> 00:40:57,902
- PDQ ASAP, you SOB.

1164
00:40:57,902 --> 00:41:00,402
(tense music)

1165
00:41:09,230 --> 00:41:10,586
- Here, you'd better put these on.

1166
00:41:10,586 --> 00:41:11,840
- I suggest you spray that whole cavern

1167
00:41:11,840 --> 00:41:14,125
so it'll get it when it comes back.

1168
00:41:14,125 --> 00:41:16,958
- Hey, who ordered the Hunan beef?

1169
00:41:20,323 --> 00:41:23,406
- They all look like Spy Vs. Spy now.

1170
00:41:27,548 --> 00:41:30,381
- All right, get in the clock now.

1171
00:41:41,728 --> 00:41:42,783
All right, you jump in there.

1172
00:41:42,783 --> 00:41:43,950
You die first.

1173
00:41:49,882 --> 00:41:50,986
- You know, this is great.

1174
00:41:50,986 --> 00:41:53,170
It's really quite comfortable.

1175
00:41:53,170 --> 00:41:54,836
It's nice.

1176
00:41:54,836 --> 00:41:55,669
- Get down here.

1177
00:41:55,669 --> 00:41:58,669
You have got to be kidding me, Pyle.

1178
00:42:01,244 --> 00:42:02,784
- You know, those guys
are pretty much toast,

1179
00:42:02,784 --> 00:42:05,867
or, you know, a light snack at least.

1180
00:42:06,750 --> 00:42:09,000
(mumbling)

1181
00:42:10,491 --> 00:42:12,853
(spraying)

1182
00:42:12,853 --> 00:42:14,787
(screeching)

1183
00:42:14,787 --> 00:42:17,037
- Sounds like Jim Morrison.

1184
00:42:20,100 --> 00:42:21,434
(chuckling)

1185
00:42:21,434 --> 00:42:24,517
- I love it when my food's delivered.

1186
00:42:30,210 --> 00:42:32,591
(imitating spider screeching)

1187
00:42:32,591 --> 00:42:33,424
- Uh-oh.

1188
00:42:34,753 --> 00:42:36,982
- Help me, help me!

1189
00:42:36,982 --> 00:42:39,135
- No, Dr. Earhart, no!

1190
00:42:39,135 --> 00:42:40,950
- So that's what happened to him.

1191
00:42:40,950 --> 00:42:42,573
- Wow.

1192
00:42:42,573 --> 00:42:43,406
- Enjoy.

1193
00:42:46,771 --> 00:42:49,714
(screaming)

1194
00:42:49,714 --> 00:42:52,279
- Oh, I'll get a giant
paper towel and smush it.

1195
00:42:52,279 --> 00:42:55,152
- Ugh.
- Not him, the spider.

1196
00:42:55,152 --> 00:42:56,774
- He died as he lived,

1197
00:42:56,774 --> 00:42:59,191
with jelly all over his face.

1198
00:43:04,338 --> 00:43:07,793
- Oh, everybody would have left him.

1199
00:43:07,793 --> 00:43:10,460
(intense music)

1200
00:43:14,129 --> 00:43:15,923
Oh, it's been a big day.

1201
00:43:15,923 --> 00:43:17,506
I'm tired.
- Mm-hmm.

1202
00:43:22,301 --> 00:43:23,967
- Carol?
- Hey, hey, I've got her.

1203
00:43:23,967 --> 00:43:24,800
She's mine.

1204
00:43:24,800 --> 00:43:27,455
- You think it'd be better if
I talked to your mother first?

1205
00:43:27,455 --> 00:43:28,683
- No, thanks.

1206
00:43:28,683 --> 00:43:29,997
I can do it.

1207
00:43:29,997 --> 00:43:32,580
- I'm ready to believe you can.

1208
00:43:33,424 --> 00:43:35,804
- The box with the bracelet.

1209
00:43:35,804 --> 00:43:37,289
- Are you sure you had it?

1210
00:43:37,289 --> 00:43:38,122
- Better go back.

1211
00:43:38,122 --> 00:43:39,935
- It was in this pocket.

1212
00:43:39,935 --> 00:43:42,119
Maybe I dropped it in there.
- You can't go back there now.

1213
00:43:42,119 --> 00:43:43,327
The place is full of gas.

1214
00:43:43,327 --> 00:43:44,160
- That was me.

1215
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:45,685
I'm sorry.

1216
00:43:45,685 --> 00:43:49,268
- We'll come back some
other time together.

1217
00:43:52,073 --> 00:43:54,217
- Nice shooting, Mr. Carson.

1218
00:43:54,217 --> 00:43:56,401
- Hey, Haskell, when you get back to town,

1219
00:43:56,401 --> 00:43:58,320
tell old Warren to get right out here

1220
00:43:58,320 --> 00:43:59,783
and board this entrance up.

1221
00:43:59,783 --> 00:44:00,866
- Right, sir.

1222
00:44:03,150 --> 00:44:03,983
- Well, if you do,

1223
00:44:03,983 --> 00:44:06,131
I suggest you put a big wide door in it.

1224
00:44:06,131 --> 00:44:07,289
The whole world is going
to want to come here

1225
00:44:07,289 --> 00:44:10,188
and take a look at that thing down there.

1226
00:44:10,188 --> 00:44:11,511
- What's your idea that
- Water slide maybe?

1227
00:44:11,511 --> 00:44:13,132
the town should charge admission?

1228
00:44:13,132 --> 00:44:15,272
- That isn't what I was thinking of.

1229
00:44:15,272 --> 00:44:16,985
You know what we egg-heads
are like, Sheriff.

1230
00:44:16,985 --> 00:44:17,986
We want to know why this,

1231
00:44:17,986 --> 00:44:18,819
how come that,

1232
00:44:18,819 --> 00:44:19,652
what about the other?

1233
00:44:19,652 --> 00:44:20,485
- Can't finish a sentence.

1234
00:44:20,485 --> 00:44:21,318
- It's a matter of scientific interest

1235
00:44:21,318 --> 00:44:22,507
to find out what made that creature.

1236
00:44:22,507 --> 00:44:24,196
- Hey, you got some deputy on your face.

1237
00:44:24,196 --> 00:44:25,029
- It's too late now.

1238
00:44:25,029 --> 00:44:25,862
It'd dead.

1239
00:44:25,862 --> 00:44:26,714
- The spider is,

1240
00:44:26,714 --> 00:44:28,533
but not the principle
that caused it to grow.

1241
00:44:28,533 --> 00:44:30,879
That's still for us to discover.

1242
00:44:30,879 --> 00:44:32,233
- Well why bother?

1243
00:44:32,233 --> 00:44:33,786
- We have to put an end to it.

1244
00:44:33,786 --> 00:44:35,294
Otherwise there may be more giant spiders

1245
00:44:35,294 --> 00:44:36,742
coming into the world.

1246
00:44:36,742 --> 00:44:37,917
- They may even be
hatching from their eggs

1247
00:44:37,917 --> 00:44:39,948
in some remote spot right now.

1248
00:44:39,948 --> 00:44:41,229
Do you realize how easy it would be

1249
00:44:41,229 --> 00:44:43,676
for them to overcome us humans?

1250
00:44:43,676 --> 00:44:44,620
And instead of being the hunters,

1251
00:44:44,620 --> 00:44:45,691
we'd become the hunted.

1252
00:44:45,691 --> 00:44:46,620
- Heavy.

1253
00:44:46,620 --> 00:44:47,453
- They'd be our masters.

1254
00:44:47,453 --> 00:44:49,242
They'd live on us.

1255
00:44:49,242 --> 00:44:51,073
- What would you suggest
the Sheriff's Office

1256
00:44:51,073 --> 00:44:53,281
of River Falls do about it, Mr. Kingman?

1257
00:44:53,281 --> 00:44:54,339
- Close the beaches.

1258
00:44:54,339 --> 00:44:55,903
- Bring that spider up into the daylight.

1259
00:44:55,903 --> 00:44:58,467
- Time to go, guys.
- Have him studied properly.

1260
00:44:58,467 --> 00:45:01,614
- Now, if you want to do it,
go ahead and leave me out.

1261
00:45:01,614 --> 00:45:02,720
It's not my job,

1262
00:45:02,720 --> 00:45:04,158
and I don't want any part of it.

1263
00:45:04,158 --> 00:45:05,491
- Okay, Sheriff.

1264
00:45:07,975 --> 00:45:10,558
(upbeat music)

1265
00:45:14,197 --> 00:45:15,614
- We are Spidoor!

1266
00:45:17,267 --> 00:45:18,684
- This is going to be really cool.

1267
00:45:18,684 --> 00:45:20,328
- On the count of four.

1268
00:45:20,328 --> 00:45:23,226
Let's wail, one, two, three, four!

1269
00:45:23,226 --> 00:45:25,309
- Let's rock this mother!

1270
00:45:26,157 --> 00:45:27,930
- Hold, hold on, Gypsy.

1271
00:45:27,930 --> 00:45:30,297
This isn't some frivolous,
stupid bubblegum music

1272
00:45:30,297 --> 00:45:31,387
we're trying to play.

1273
00:45:31,387 --> 00:45:33,333
This is an ambitious rock opera.

1274
00:45:33,333 --> 00:45:35,784
- Yeah, kind of like what
Kiss would play, you know?

1275
00:45:35,784 --> 00:45:38,345
- Joel, Joel, Joel, I got news for you.

1276
00:45:38,345 --> 00:45:39,623
Kiss were never cool.

1277
00:45:39,623 --> 00:45:43,016
- Well, everybody back at
Ashwaubenon High School

1278
00:45:43,016 --> 00:45:44,177
used to love Kiss.

1279
00:45:44,177 --> 00:45:45,674
- Great, you spent your adolescence

1280
00:45:45,674 --> 00:45:46,790
with a bunch of geeky losers,

1281
00:45:46,790 --> 00:45:48,124
and now it's our cross to bear?

1282
00:45:48,124 --> 00:45:49,642
- Nobody calls Ashwaubenon Jaguars

1283
00:45:49,642 --> 00:45:50,709
a bunch of geeky losers.

1284
00:45:50,709 --> 00:45:51,542
- Come on, Joel.

1285
00:45:51,542 --> 00:45:53,433
We were thinking our
rock opera would be more

1286
00:45:53,433 --> 00:45:55,504
along the lines of '70s
progressive rock bands,

1287
00:45:55,504 --> 00:45:58,541
like Yes, Gentle Giant, King Crimson,

1288
00:45:58,541 --> 00:46:00,176
Emerson Lake and Palmer.

1289
00:46:00,176 --> 00:46:02,246
(sobbing)

1290
00:46:02,246 --> 00:46:03,079
- What's with her?

1291
00:46:03,079 --> 00:46:03,912
- Take it easy.

1292
00:46:03,912 --> 00:46:04,745
That's okay, honey.

1293
00:46:04,745 --> 00:46:08,456
Don't ever mention Emerson
Lake and Palmer around Gypsy.

1294
00:46:08,456 --> 00:46:09,475
Take it easy.

1295
00:46:09,475 --> 00:46:10,308
That's okay.

1296
00:46:10,308 --> 00:46:11,499
Okay, let's wail you guys.

1297
00:46:11,499 --> 00:46:12,421
- Spidoor!

1298
00:46:12,421 --> 00:46:13,838
- We are Spidoor!

1299
00:46:15,252 --> 00:46:17,343
One, two, three, four!

1300
00:46:17,343 --> 00:46:19,701
(screaming)

1301
00:46:19,701 --> 00:46:23,201
Quick, Cambot, give me Rocket Number Nine.

1302
00:46:27,831 --> 00:46:30,306
- What the Hector Alonzo's going on?

1303
00:46:30,306 --> 00:46:31,732
What's all that bracket down there?

1304
00:46:31,732 --> 00:46:32,565
- Well it's just us.

1305
00:46:32,565 --> 00:46:34,261
We're getting ready to
play our rock opera.

1306
00:46:34,261 --> 00:46:36,525
- You don't have a permit
for electronic instruments.

1307
00:46:36,525 --> 00:46:37,358
I'm sorry.

1308
00:46:37,358 --> 00:46:38,758
Nobody said anything about that.

1309
00:46:38,758 --> 00:46:40,230
- Hey, who are you anyway, Pops?

1310
00:46:40,230 --> 00:46:42,208
- I'm from the planet Xephelodian.

1311
00:46:42,208 --> 00:46:44,013
We provide maintenance
and janitorial services

1312
00:46:44,013 --> 00:46:44,954
for the universe.

1313
00:46:44,954 --> 00:46:47,473
I'm the custodian of the Seventh Galaxy.

1314
00:46:47,473 --> 00:46:49,911
- Wow, a custodian from the Seventh Galaxy

1315
00:46:49,911 --> 00:46:51,441
is from the planet Xephelodian.

1316
00:46:51,441 --> 00:46:53,097
He's perfect for our rock opera!

1317
00:46:53,097 --> 00:46:55,086
- You have got to be kidding me.

1318
00:46:55,086 --> 00:46:56,212
A rock opera?

1319
00:46:56,212 --> 00:46:57,513
Is it femme like Kiss,

1320
00:46:57,513 --> 00:46:58,835
or is it boring and pretentious

1321
00:46:58,835 --> 00:47:00,629
like Emerson Lake and Palmer?

1322
00:47:00,629 --> 00:47:02,377
(groaning, sobbing)

1323
00:47:02,377 --> 00:47:03,210
- Oh no, Gypsy.

1324
00:47:03,210 --> 00:47:05,530
Brain salad surgery.

1325
00:47:05,530 --> 00:47:06,634
- Hey, don't blame me.

1326
00:47:06,634 --> 00:47:07,467
I didn't do it.

1327
00:47:07,467 --> 00:47:09,348
- Don't worry, I'll beam
some sawdust over to you.

1328
00:47:09,348 --> 00:47:10,773
Just let me get it out of my locker.

1329
00:47:10,773 --> 00:47:11,865
Check my keys here.

1330
00:47:11,865 --> 00:47:13,028
Oh, I got to go.

1331
00:47:13,028 --> 00:47:14,945
There's a pep rally on Trielphamagore.

1332
00:47:14,945 --> 00:47:16,076
I got to get a good seat.

1333
00:47:16,076 --> 00:47:16,909
Beat it.

1334
00:47:16,909 --> 00:47:18,075
Quiet for God's sake.

1335
00:47:18,075 --> 00:47:19,158
Keep it down.

1336
00:47:19,158 --> 00:47:20,788
- Great, now we've got
to clean up Gypsy's mess.

1337
00:47:20,788 --> 00:47:21,903
Geez, those high school janitors

1338
00:47:21,903 --> 00:47:23,549
are always so weird and creepy.

1339
00:47:23,549 --> 00:47:25,975
- We should chip in and buy
him something for Christmas,

1340
00:47:25,975 --> 00:47:26,848
you know?

1341
00:47:26,848 --> 00:47:28,655
- Yeah, actually that
stuff would go really good

1342
00:47:28,655 --> 00:47:31,238
with our new Spidoor stage set.

1343
00:47:32,507 --> 00:47:33,340
Let's wail!

1344
00:47:33,340 --> 00:47:34,209
Spidoors are go!

1345
00:47:34,209 --> 00:47:35,798
One, two, three, four!

1346
00:47:35,798 --> 00:47:39,051
(alarm blaring, screaming)

1347
00:47:39,051 --> 00:47:42,218
(doors slamming open)

1348
00:47:48,619 --> 00:47:51,332
I'd like to know what that
janitor has to do with anything.

1349
00:47:51,332 --> 00:47:52,379
- We were just about wail.

1350
00:47:52,379 --> 00:47:53,462
- Oh, thanks.

1351
00:47:54,748 --> 00:47:55,737
- Ooh, those things chafe.

1352
00:47:55,737 --> 00:47:57,266
- Usually if nature produces a freak,

1353
00:47:57,266 --> 00:47:58,739
it dies immediately,

1354
00:47:58,739 --> 00:48:00,751
because it can't adapt itself to life.

1355
00:48:00,751 --> 00:48:02,913
- Kind of like Hudson Hawk.

1356
00:48:02,913 --> 00:48:04,845
- It's what we ordinarily
call a bird spider.

1357
00:48:04,845 --> 00:48:07,745
- Yeah, this one's called
a big flipping bird spider.

1358
00:48:07,745 --> 00:48:09,992
- Perfect phrases.

1359
00:48:09,992 --> 00:48:11,992
Perfect in every detail.

1360
00:48:12,963 --> 00:48:14,146
- What's the matter with spiders.

1361
00:48:14,146 --> 00:48:16,313
- On the floor, four eyes.

1362
00:48:17,885 --> 00:48:19,528
- Fraser, are you hurt?

1363
00:48:19,528 --> 00:48:21,163
- Come on, Joe, are you
going to take that from him?

1364
00:48:21,163 --> 00:48:22,485
He's just a big dumb spider.

1365
00:48:22,485 --> 00:48:23,359
Come on.

1366
00:48:23,359 --> 00:48:25,107
- I'm sure it's dead.

1367
00:48:25,107 --> 00:48:26,717
- How come it kicked me?

1368
00:48:26,717 --> 00:48:28,373
- It's just a muscular contraction.

1369
00:48:28,373 --> 00:48:29,535
Davanni's reaction it's called.

1370
00:48:29,535 --> 00:48:31,246
It often happens.

1371
00:48:31,246 --> 00:48:33,353
- Not to me, it doesn't.

1372
00:48:33,353 --> 00:48:35,009
If it can do that when it's dead,

1373
00:48:35,009 --> 00:48:37,192
I'd sure hate to meet it somewhere alive.

1374
00:48:37,192 --> 00:48:40,025
- You know, you are funny, Friend.

1375
00:48:42,909 --> 00:48:44,092
- I don't think he's funny.

1376
00:48:44,092 --> 00:48:46,739
Daddy's dead and everyone's making jokes.

1377
00:48:46,739 --> 00:48:48,717
- This music's making me retch, Mom.

1378
00:48:48,717 --> 00:48:49,855
Turn it off.

1379
00:48:49,855 --> 00:48:51,072
- Oh, still upset about Dad?

1380
00:48:51,072 --> 00:48:52,396
That gutter snipe would have traded you

1381
00:48:52,396 --> 00:48:54,384
for a case of German beer any old day.

1382
00:48:54,384 --> 00:48:55,960
- I can't help it, Mother.

1383
00:48:55,960 --> 00:48:56,793
- I know.

1384
00:48:58,469 --> 00:48:59,837
I loved him too.

1385
00:48:59,837 --> 00:49:02,308
- Sometimes, like the day he was sober.

1386
00:49:02,308 --> 00:49:04,366
- I keep thinking it was my fault.

1387
00:49:04,366 --> 00:49:05,286
- Well, granted.

1388
00:49:05,286 --> 00:49:06,786
- How could it be?

1389
00:49:07,862 --> 00:49:08,695
- It never would have happened

1390
00:49:08,695 --> 00:49:11,002
if he hadn't gone to get me that present.

1391
00:49:11,002 --> 00:49:12,244
- Good point.

1392
00:49:12,244 --> 00:49:13,695
- I've lost him.

1393
00:49:13,695 --> 00:49:16,016
- You mustn't think of it that way, Carol.

1394
00:49:16,016 --> 00:49:18,222
- Well, that's all broken up.

1395
00:49:18,222 --> 00:49:20,537
- Drop it in that cave.

1396
00:49:20,537 --> 00:49:23,079
I wanted so much to keep it, Mother.

1397
00:49:23,079 --> 00:49:24,986
- Forget about it now, dear.

1398
00:49:24,986 --> 00:49:27,069
You have your homework to
get ready for tomorrow.

1399
00:49:27,069 --> 00:49:28,691
- I don't want you getting bad grades

1400
00:49:28,691 --> 00:49:31,024
just 'cause Dad's worm food.

1401
00:49:32,264 --> 00:49:33,429
- Oh, and I forgot.

1402
00:49:33,429 --> 00:49:36,221
Mike called you twice before
you got home from school,

1403
00:49:36,221 --> 00:49:38,129
and said he would be at
his father's theater.

1404
00:49:38,129 --> 00:49:39,167
- His father!

1405
00:49:39,167 --> 00:49:41,750
(mock sobbing)

1406
00:49:44,496 --> 00:49:47,079
(gentle music)

1407
00:49:51,001 --> 00:49:53,494
- Dial 1-900-Boring-Boyfriend.

1408
00:49:53,494 --> 00:49:56,001
He'd love to talk to you about
his uncle from Milwaukee.

1409
00:49:56,001 --> 00:49:57,001
- Right now.

1410
00:49:58,658 --> 00:49:59,854
- Hello, Mr. Simpson.

1411
00:49:59,854 --> 00:50:01,437
Can I talk to Mike?

1412
00:50:02,844 --> 00:50:05,523
- Hey, what kind of sin must a man commit

1413
00:50:05,523 --> 00:50:07,023
in a single night.

1414
00:50:09,346 --> 00:50:10,238
- Mike?

1415
00:50:10,238 --> 00:50:12,490
You're wanted on the phone.

1416
00:50:12,490 --> 00:50:14,501
- And I get my shirts from the laundry.

1417
00:50:14,501 --> 00:50:16,300
(chuckling)

1418
00:50:16,300 --> 00:50:20,717
- Oh boy, I get to walk past
that poster again, cool.

1419
00:50:26,413 --> 00:50:27,496
- Everywhere.

1420
00:50:29,732 --> 00:50:30,929
- Hi, Mike.

1421
00:50:30,929 --> 00:50:32,096
This is Carol.

1422
00:50:32,977 --> 00:50:35,474
You said you'd take me out to
the cave to find the bracelet.

1423
00:50:35,474 --> 00:50:36,307
- I lied.

1424
00:50:36,307 --> 00:50:37,565
- You don't mean right now, do you?

1425
00:50:37,565 --> 00:50:39,405
- If you can get away.

1426
00:50:39,405 --> 00:50:40,567
Please, Mike?

1427
00:50:40,567 --> 00:50:41,400
- Carol, not today.

1428
00:50:41,400 --> 00:50:42,659
My dad just got in a new picture

1429
00:50:42,659 --> 00:50:44,499
and I haven't even seen it yet.

1430
00:50:44,499 --> 00:50:45,673
Something about puppet people.

1431
00:50:45,673 --> 00:50:47,153
It sounds pretty wild.

1432
00:50:47,153 --> 00:50:47,986
- Please?

1433
00:50:47,986 --> 00:50:50,294
- Shame on you, Bert I. Gordon.

1434
00:50:50,294 --> 00:50:51,127
- Okay, Carol.

1435
00:50:51,127 --> 00:50:53,322
I think I can borrow Joe's car again.

1436
00:50:53,322 --> 00:50:56,196
- Only don't come by the house for me.

1437
00:50:56,196 --> 00:50:58,842
I don't want Mother to
know I'm going out there.

1438
00:50:58,842 --> 00:50:59,675
- All right, I'll be,

1439
00:50:59,675 --> 00:51:01,773
I'll be down at the corner in 15 minutes.

1440
00:51:01,773 --> 00:51:03,273
- I'll be waiting.

1441
00:51:05,580 --> 00:51:06,413
- I'll see ya, Dad.

1442
00:51:06,413 --> 00:51:08,397
I got a date now.

1443
00:51:08,397 --> 00:51:09,764
- Where will you be?

1444
00:51:09,764 --> 00:51:11,584
I said, where would you be if I want you?

1445
00:51:11,584 --> 00:51:12,710
- Wait, aren't you going to watch

1446
00:51:12,710 --> 00:51:16,366
that wonderful Bert I. Gordon movie?

1447
00:51:16,366 --> 00:51:19,199
- It looks like an ad for Dockers.

1448
00:51:21,887 --> 00:51:22,720
- Old high school.

1449
00:51:22,720 --> 00:51:23,594
- Hey, Joe!

1450
00:51:23,594 --> 00:51:25,671
- Hey, where are you going
with that gun in your hand?

1451
00:51:25,671 --> 00:51:26,504
- Hi.

1452
00:51:26,504 --> 00:51:29,223
- Hey, you remember that
five bucks I loaned you?

1453
00:51:29,223 --> 00:51:30,443
- Sure, it was last Christmas

1454
00:51:30,443 --> 00:51:31,293
and I paid it back.

1455
00:51:31,293 --> 00:51:33,399
- All I mean is I'm your friend.

1456
00:51:33,399 --> 00:51:34,232
You know that, don't you?

1457
00:51:34,232 --> 00:51:35,155
- You want to borrow five bucks is all?

1458
00:51:35,155 --> 00:51:35,988
- No, no.

1459
00:51:37,089 --> 00:51:39,527
Could Carol and I use your
wagon for a couple of hours?

1460
00:51:39,527 --> 00:51:40,360
- Again?

1461
00:51:41,469 --> 00:51:42,940
What's the big deal?

1462
00:51:42,940 --> 00:51:45,059
- What do I have sucker stamped my head?

1463
00:51:45,059 --> 00:51:45,991
- Look, we're friends, Joe.

1464
00:51:45,991 --> 00:51:46,824
Just remember that.

1465
00:51:46,824 --> 00:51:47,681
- Why don't you come to the rehearsal?

1466
00:51:47,681 --> 00:51:48,819
- Look, my mouth is getting tired.

1467
00:51:48,819 --> 00:51:49,889
Are we friends or aren't we?

1468
00:51:49,889 --> 00:51:50,901
- Okay, okay.

1469
00:51:50,901 --> 00:51:51,820
The car's in the driveway.

1470
00:51:51,820 --> 00:51:52,683
You'll find the keys in-

1471
00:51:52,683 --> 00:51:53,547
- The glove compartment.

1472
00:51:53,547 --> 00:51:54,408
Where else?

1473
00:51:54,408 --> 00:51:55,903
- Why do I have so many friends?

1474
00:51:55,903 --> 00:51:56,789
- Thanks, Joe.

1475
00:51:56,789 --> 00:51:58,065
- Hey, that's Harvey Keitel.

1476
00:51:58,065 --> 00:51:58,975
- Oh, the pain.

1477
00:51:58,975 --> 00:51:59,808
- Come on, Joe.

1478
00:51:59,808 --> 00:52:01,296
We've got problems.

1479
00:52:01,296 --> 00:52:02,523
No show today, it's off limits.

1480
00:52:02,523 --> 00:52:04,690
- Maybe it's a frat party?

1481
00:52:09,065 --> 00:52:10,257
- But we've got to get in.

1482
00:52:10,257 --> 00:52:11,973
The dance is tomorrow night.

1483
00:52:11,973 --> 00:52:14,168
Other cats will have a blast
if we don't swing solid.

1484
00:52:14,168 --> 00:52:15,918
- Yeah, what he said.

1485
00:52:17,321 --> 00:52:18,459
- Hey, let's get the janitor.

1486
00:52:18,459 --> 00:52:19,292
- Janitor.

1487
00:52:19,292 --> 00:52:20,125
- Janitor.

1488
00:52:20,125 --> 00:52:22,206
- That's how it fits in.

1489
00:52:22,206 --> 00:52:25,451
- They can't hang us for trying, can they?

1490
00:52:25,451 --> 00:52:27,082
Hugo?

1491
00:52:27,082 --> 00:52:27,915
Hey, Hugo!

1492
00:52:27,915 --> 00:52:28,748
- Hugo!

1493
00:52:28,748 --> 00:52:29,581
- Looking for his car?

1494
00:52:29,581 --> 00:52:31,445
- Who's calling Hugo?

1495
00:52:31,445 --> 00:52:32,278
- There he is.

1496
00:52:32,278 --> 00:52:33,296
- Well now, what's the matter here?

1497
00:52:33,296 --> 00:52:34,129
- Someone threw up.

1498
00:52:34,129 --> 00:52:35,469
Got some sawdust?
- We want in.

1499
00:52:35,469 --> 00:52:37,355
- We've got to develop our talents.

1500
00:52:37,355 --> 00:52:39,829
- You'll have to use the
auditorium til further notice.

1501
00:52:39,829 --> 00:52:41,163
Principal's orders.

1502
00:52:41,163 --> 00:52:42,255
- No can do.

1503
00:52:42,255 --> 00:52:43,634
The drama class is there.

1504
00:52:43,634 --> 00:52:44,667
- I can't help that.

1505
00:52:44,667 --> 00:52:45,647
Thing here is tricking up.

1506
00:52:45,647 --> 00:52:48,533
Told me particular not to let nobody in.

1507
00:52:48,533 --> 00:52:49,366
- Particular.

1508
00:52:49,366 --> 00:52:50,304
- Well, he didn't mean us.

1509
00:52:50,304 --> 00:52:51,605
He met squares.

1510
00:52:51,605 --> 00:52:53,605
We're the coolest zoologists in town.

1511
00:52:53,605 --> 00:52:54,800
- No sirree.

1512
00:52:54,800 --> 00:52:56,987
The moving people are coming here tonight

1513
00:52:56,987 --> 00:52:58,710
to move that thing to the university.

1514
00:52:58,710 --> 00:52:59,919
- Help me find my chin.

1515
00:52:59,919 --> 00:53:01,411
- Want to do afterwards.

1516
00:53:01,411 --> 00:53:02,804
- In the dead of night?

1517
00:53:02,804 --> 00:53:05,088
Hugo, we're only little kids.
- What are you talking about?

1518
00:53:05,088 --> 00:53:06,059
You're 42.

1519
00:53:06,059 --> 00:53:07,559
- Well, all right.

1520
00:53:08,555 --> 00:53:09,635
Your mom's first.

1521
00:53:09,635 --> 00:53:10,468
- Rubbish.

1522
00:53:10,468 --> 00:53:13,363
- Now I want you to behave yourself.

1523
00:53:13,363 --> 00:53:15,835
Don't monkey with that thing.

1524
00:53:15,835 --> 00:53:16,927
- Dig that?

1525
00:53:16,927 --> 00:53:18,687
Now the first guy who
steps on daddy long legs

1526
00:53:18,687 --> 00:53:20,343
is going to hear from Hugo!

1527
00:53:20,343 --> 00:53:21,176
- Yeah.

1528
00:53:21,176 --> 00:53:25,633
- Yeah, I got to go play
with my pig, Arnold.

1529
00:53:25,633 --> 00:53:27,347
(tense music)

1530
00:53:27,347 --> 00:53:28,180
- Say.

1531
00:53:35,855 --> 00:53:38,225
- Imagine if they had the
budget to show it all.

1532
00:53:38,225 --> 00:53:39,948
(whistling)

1533
00:53:39,948 --> 00:53:44,653
- Look, spider, all of us got
together and voted you out.

1534
00:53:44,653 --> 00:53:46,848
- It's big, all right.

1535
00:53:46,848 --> 00:53:48,655
- Hey, maybe the drama class
is through with the auditorium.

1536
00:53:48,655 --> 00:53:49,495
Let's blow this place.

1537
00:53:49,495 --> 00:53:50,817
It gives me the creeps.

1538
00:53:50,817 --> 00:53:51,908
- What is it, Superman?

1539
00:53:51,908 --> 00:53:52,807
Chickening out?

1540
00:53:52,807 --> 00:53:54,058
- Well, certainly not.

1541
00:53:54,058 --> 00:53:57,490
- Well then, stop goofing off, huh?

1542
00:53:57,490 --> 00:53:59,907
- That's a nice process shot.

1543
00:54:06,490 --> 00:54:09,953
(playing piano)

1544
00:54:09,953 --> 00:54:11,357
Okay, we'll open with Rite of Spring

1545
00:54:11,357 --> 00:54:12,875
and then kick into Blue Suede Shoes.

1546
00:54:12,875 --> 00:54:14,461
- All right, let's start
with the opening number.

1547
00:54:14,461 --> 00:54:16,012
And don't go to sleep on it.

1548
00:54:16,012 --> 00:54:17,300
Play with everything you've got.

1549
00:54:17,300 --> 00:54:19,954
- Especially the instruments.

1550
00:54:19,954 --> 00:54:20,787
- Sorry.

1551
00:54:22,569 --> 00:54:24,216
- Play good and loud.

1552
00:54:24,216 --> 00:54:25,858
- More rock bands could use conductors.

1553
00:54:25,858 --> 00:54:26,691
- Okay!

1554
00:54:26,691 --> 00:54:29,414
(screaming)

1555
00:54:29,414 --> 00:54:32,036
- It's the New Christy Minstrels.

1556
00:54:32,036 --> 00:54:33,379
- Hey, what goes on here?

1557
00:54:33,379 --> 00:54:35,393
- It's the drama class, what else?

1558
00:54:35,393 --> 00:54:37,439
- All right, either come in or get out.

1559
00:54:37,439 --> 00:54:38,991
Don't stand there and scream.

1560
00:54:38,991 --> 00:54:39,824
We're rehearsing.

1561
00:54:39,824 --> 00:54:41,935
- Theater freaks.

1562
00:54:41,935 --> 00:54:43,648
- One, two, three, four.

1563
00:54:43,648 --> 00:54:44,481
- Four and a half.

1564
00:54:44,481 --> 00:54:46,789
(rock and roll music)

1565
00:54:46,789 --> 00:54:48,090
- Missed the downbeat.

1566
00:54:48,090 --> 00:54:50,007
- Well, okay, let's go.

1567
00:54:51,727 --> 00:54:53,010
(yelling in surprise)

1568
00:54:53,010 --> 00:54:55,128
- Oh, they walked right through us.

1569
00:54:55,128 --> 00:54:56,211
That's weird.

1570
00:55:03,429 --> 00:55:05,990
- Now he's doing his Charlie Callas.

1571
00:55:05,990 --> 00:55:09,240
I think this guy peaked in high school.

1572
00:55:11,813 --> 00:55:13,313
- Come on, boogie.

1573
00:55:14,448 --> 00:55:15,723
Everybody, come on.

1574
00:55:15,723 --> 00:55:17,183
- Oh, now they're Voguing.

1575
00:55:17,183 --> 00:55:19,278
- Yep, strike a pose.

1576
00:55:19,278 --> 00:55:20,369
- Geez, I hate this music.

1577
00:55:20,369 --> 00:55:22,038
Don't you know any "Spider" John Koerner?

1578
00:55:22,038 --> 00:55:22,871
Come on.

1579
00:55:23,991 --> 00:55:25,824
- Hey, swing it, cats.

1580
00:55:26,947 --> 00:55:30,456
Ooh, you know, you should
really get that guy on a stool.

1581
00:55:30,456 --> 00:55:31,363
- Hike up your pants now.

1582
00:55:31,363 --> 00:55:32,995
